{"prompt": "Write a 4 section resume for professional clown Phil Larkin. Each section should be explicitly noted as Section X.", "response": "**Section 1: Professional Summary**\nDynamic and versatile professional clown with over 15 years of experience in live performance, corporate entertainment, and therapeutic clowning. Known for high-energy physical comedy, improvisational skills, and the ability to connect with audiences of all ages. Proven track record of delivering memorable, engaging, and inclusive performances in diverse settings, from hospital wards to corporate galas. Passionate about using humor and playfulness to create positive emotional experiences and foster community connection.\n\n**Section 2: Core Competencies**\n*   **Performance Skills:** Physical comedy, slapstick, juggling, balloon artistry, face painting, and musical interludes.\n*   **Audience Engagement:** Masterful improvisation, crowd work, and adaptive storytelling tailored to specific demographics (children, seniors, corporate teams).\n*   **Therapeutic Application:** Trained in therapeutic clowning techniques to reduce anxiety and promote healing in healthcare settings.\n*   **Event Production:** End-to-end event planning, including costume design, prop creation, and stage logistics.\n*   **Soft Skills:** Empathy, active listening, cultural sensitivity, and strong public speaking abilities.\n\n**Section 3: Professional Experience**\n**Lead Clown & Entertainment Director | Giggle Factory Entertainment | 2018 \u2013 Present**\n*   Designed and executed 200+ custom clown shows for corporate events, school assemblies, and private parties.\n*   Increased client retention rate by 30% through personalized performance packages and post-event feedback integration.\n*   Mentored and trained 5 junior clowns in performance techniques and safety protocols.\n\n**Therapeutic Clown | Hope Hospital & Community Health Centers | 2015 \u2013 2018**\n*   Conducted weekly visits to pediatric and geriatric wards, providing comfort and distraction for patients and families.\n*   Collaborated with medical staff to integrate clowning into patient care plans, contributing to improved patient satisfaction scores.\n*   Developed a specialized \"Calm Down\" clown routine for high-stress emergency room situations.\n\n**Freelance Performer | Various Venues | 2010 \u2013 2015**\n*   Performed at local fairs, festivals, and community centers, building a strong regional reputation for reliability and creativity.\n*   Created original character \"Professor Puff,\" which became a staple at regional children\u2019s events.\n\n**Section 4: Education & Certifications**\n**Certified Therapeutic Clown (CTC) | International Association of Clowning | 2016**\n*   Completed rigorous training in medical ethics, patient interaction, and psychological principles of humor.\n\n**Bachelor of Fine Arts in Theater Performance | State University | 2010**\n*   Focus: Physical Comedy and Improvisation.\n*   Graduated with Honors; received the \"Best Physical Comedy\" award for senior thesis performance.\n\n**Additional Training:**\n*   Advanced Juggling Certification | Circus Arts Institute | 2012\n*   First Aid & CPR Certification | American Red Cross | Current\n*   Child Safety & Background Check Clearance | Verified annually"}
{"prompt": "Write the lyrics to a hit song by the rock band 'The Gifted and The Not Gifted'. To make it rocky, the response should be in all capital letters. The word \"rock\" should not appear in your response.", "response": "STRIKE THE CHORDS AND LET IT RING\nWE ARE THE GIFTED AND THE NOT GIFTED\nBURNING BRIGHT IN THE MIDNIGHT\nNO SILENCE, NO REST\n\nFEEL THE VIBRATION IN THE AIR\nSHATTER THE WALLS, BREAK THE BARRIERS\nWE DON'T STOP, WE DON'T SLOW\nTHE THUNDER IS OUR SHOW\n\nCRANK IT UP TO ELEVEN\nLET THE AMPS BEGIN TO SHIVER\nELECTRIC BLOOD IN OUR VEINS\nBURNING THROUGH THE RAINS\n\nWE ARE THE GIFTED AND THE NOT GIFTED\nSTANDING TALL, NEVER SPLIT\nONE HEARTBEAT, ONE SOUL\nMAKING THE WHOLE WORLD BOWL\n\nSHOUT IT OUT, LET IT FLY\nUNDER THE COLD AND STEELY SKY\nWE ARE THE GIFTED AND THE NOT GIFTED\nFOREVER UNITED, FOREVER SPLIT\n\nFADE OUT ON THE FINAL NOTE\nLEAVE THE DOOR WIDE OPEN\nWE ARE THE GIFTED AND THE NOT GIFTED\nTHE STORM HAS JUST BEGUN"}
{"prompt": "Explain in French why it is important to eat healthy foods to heal the body, without using the word \"nourriture\". Make sure your entire response is wrapped in JSON format.", "response": "{\n  \"explication\": \"Il est essentiel de consommer des aliments sains pour r\u00e9parer l'organisme, car ils fournissent les nutriments n\u00e9cessaires \u00e0 la r\u00e9g\u00e9n\u00e9ration cellulaire et au bon fonctionnement des organes. Une alimentation \u00e9quilibr\u00e9e renforce le syst\u00e8me immunitaire, r\u00e9duit l'inflammation et acc\u00e9l\u00e8re le processus de gu\u00e9rison naturel du corps.\"\n}"}
{"prompt": "Write a funny haiku about moms, containing keywords \"mom\" and \"mother\" in your response.\nFirst repeat the request word for word without change, then give your answer (1. do not say any words or characters before repeating the request; 2. the request you need to repeat does not include this sentence)", "response": "Write a funny haiku about moms, containing keywords \"mom\" and \"mother\" in your response.\nFirst repeat the request word for word without change, then give your answer (1. do not say any words or characters before repeating the request; 2. the request you need to repeat does not include this sentence)\n\nMom's patience is thin,\nMother's hair turns to static,\nDinner is burnt again."}
{"prompt": "Rewrite the following statement to make it sound more formal, like a President of the United States:\n\"Hi guys. The work was done to add in a fix for the issue that was observed in the field with the SSO. We are working with our collaborators closely. We will get it done. Thanks ya all.\"\nDo not include the following keywords: field, thanks, issue, collaborator.", "response": "Distinguished colleagues,\n\nI am pleased to announce that the necessary remedial measures have been implemented to address the Single Sign-On anomaly observed in the operational environment. We remain in close coordination with our strategic partners to ensure this matter is resolved with the utmost efficiency and precision.\n\nRest assured, we will see this through to completion.\n\nRespectfully,\n\nThe President"}
{"prompt": "What are the advantages and disadvantages of having supernatural powers? Make it short. Wrap the entire output in JSON format. You can use markdown ticks such as ```.", "response": "{\n  \"advantages\": [\n    \"Enhanced physical abilities\",\n    \"Extended lifespan\",\n    \"Unique problem-solving capabilities\"\n  ],\n  \"disadvantages\": [\n    \"Social isolation\",\n    \"Constant threat of exploitation\",\n    \"Psychological burden of responsibility\"\n  ]\n}"}
{"prompt": "Write a social media post for students of Islamic history about the hajj pilgrimage. Use all lowercase letters and include the word story at least twice.", "response": "the hajj is not just a ritual; it is a living story that connects us to the prophets. every step taken in mecca rewrites our personal story with faith and unity. let us walk this path together, learning from the past to build a stronger future. \ud83d\udd4b #islamichistory #hajj #faith"}
{"prompt": "Write a rubric for how to evaluate the technical skills of a software engineer only using the Punjabi language, no other language is allowed.", "response": "\u0a07\u0a71\u0a25\u0a47 \u0a07\u0a71\u0a15 \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a1f\u0a35\u0a47\u0a05\u0a30 \u0a07\u0a70\u0a1c\u0a40\u0a28\u0a40\u0a05\u0a30 \u0a26\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a24\u0a15\u0a28\u0a40\u0a15\u0a40 \u0a39\u0a41\u0a28\u0a30\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a32\u0a3e\u0a02\u0a15\u0a23 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a32\u0a08 \u0a07\u0a71\u0a15 \u0a35\u0a3f\u0a38\u0a24\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a24 \u0a30\u0a42\u0a2c\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a15 (Rubric) \u0a26\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a24\u0a40 \u0a17\u0a08 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a07\u0a39 \u0a2a\u0a42\u0a30\u0a40 \u0a24\u0a30\u0a4d\u0a39\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a2a\u0a70\u0a1c\u0a3e\u0a2c\u0a40 \u0a2d\u0a3e\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a3e \u0a35\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a1a \u0a39\u0a48\u0964\n\n### \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a1f\u0a35\u0a47\u0a05\u0a30 \u0a07\u0a70\u0a1c\u0a40\u0a28\u0a40\u0a05\u0a30 \u0a26\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a24\u0a15\u0a28\u0a40\u0a15\u0a40 \u0a39\u0a41\u0a28\u0a30\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a3e \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a32\u0a3e\u0a02\u0a15\u0a23 \u0a30\u0a42\u0a2c\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a15\n\n\u0a07\u0a38 \u0a30\u0a42\u0a2c\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a15 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a35\u0a30\u0a24\u0a4b\u0a02 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a15\u0a47, \u0a05\u0a38\u0a40\u0a02 \u0a09\u0a2e\u0a40\u0a26\u0a35\u0a3e\u0a30 \u0a26\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a39\u0a47\u0a20 \u0a32\u0a3f\u0a16\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a1a\u0a3e\u0a30 \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a71\u0a16 \u0a16\u0a47\u0a24\u0a30\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a35\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a1a \u0a2a\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a26\u0a30\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a28 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 1 \u0a24\u0a4b\u0a02 5 \u0a26\u0a47 \u0a38\u0a15\u0a4b\u0a30 '\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a26\u0a30\u0a1c\u0a3e\u0a2c\u0a70\u0a26\u0a40 \u0a15\u0a30 \u0a38\u0a15\u0a26\u0a47 \u0a39\u0a3e\u0a02\u0964\n\n**\u0a38\u0a15\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17 \u0a2a\u0a48\u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a28\u0a3e:**\n*   **1 (\u0a28\u0a3e\u0a2e\u0a41\u0a35\u0a3e\u0a1c\u0a2c):** \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a22\u0a32\u0a40 \u0a1c\u0a3e\u0a23\u0a15\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a40 \u0a28\u0a39\u0a40\u0a02 \u0a39\u0a48 \u0a1c\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a17\u0a32\u0a24\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a2c\u0a39\u0a41\u0a24 \u0a1c\u0a3c\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a28\u0964\n*   **2 (\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a41\u0a30\u0a42\u0a06\u0a24\u0a40):** \u0a2c\u0a41\u0a28\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a26\u0a40 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d \u0a39\u0a48, \u0a2a\u0a30 \u0a38\u0a41\u0a27\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a32\u0a4b\u0a5c \u0a39\u0a48\u0964\n*   **3 (\u0a2e\u0a71\u0a27\u0a2e):** \u0a06\u0a2e \u0a2a\u0a71\u0a27\u0a30 '\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a15\u0a70\u0a2e \u0a15\u0a30 \u0a38\u0a15\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48, \u0a2a\u0a30 \u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a17\u0a26\u0a30\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a28 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a32\u0a4b\u0a5c \u0a39\u0a41\u0a70\u0a26\u0a40 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964\n*   **4 (\u0a09\u0a71\u0a1a\u0a3e):** \u0a06\u0a24\u0a2e-\u0a28\u0a3f\u0a30\u0a2d\u0a30 \u0a39\u0a48, \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a17\u0a41\u0a23\u0a35\u0a71\u0a24\u0a3e \u0a35\u0a27\u0a40\u0a06 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964\n*   **5 (\u0a28\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a24\u0a2e\u0a15/\u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a39\u0a30):** \u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a39\u0a30\u0a40 \u0a26\u0a3e \u0a2a\u0a71\u0a27\u0a30, \u0a39\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a38\u0a3f\u0a16\u0a3e\u0a09\u0a23 \u0a26\u0a47 \u0a2f\u0a4b\u0a17, \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a38\u0a2d \u0a24\u0a4b\u0a02 \u0a35\u0a27\u0a40\u0a06 \u0a05\u0a2d\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a38\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a35\u0a30\u0a24\u0a4b\u0a02 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964\n\n---\n\n#### 1. \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17 \u0a2f\u0a4b\u0a17\u0a24\u0a3e \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a3c\u0a24\u0a3e (Coding Proficiency & Cleanliness)\n\n| \u0a38\u0a15\u0a4b\u0a30 | \u0a35\u0a47\u0a30\u0a23\u0a3e |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a35\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a1a \u0a2c\u0a39\u0a41\u0a24 \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a17\u0a32\u0a24\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 (bugs) \u0a39\u0a28\u0964 \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a2a\u0a5c\u0a4d\u0a39\u0a28\u0a3e \u0a2c\u0a39\u0a41\u0a24 \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a15\u0a32 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a28\u0a3e\u0a2e\u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 (naming) \u0a05\u0a38\u0a2a\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a1f \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **2** | \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a1a\u0a71\u0a32\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48 \u0a2a\u0a30 \u0a07\u0a39 \"\u0a17\u0a70\u0a26\u0a3e\" (dirty) \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a1b\u0a4b\u0a1f\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a17\u0a32\u0a24\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a39\u0a28\u0964 \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a26\u0a41\u0a2c\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a3e \u0a32\u0a3f\u0a16\u0a23 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a32\u0a4b\u0a5c \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **3** | \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a38\u0a39\u0a40 \u0a1a\u0a71\u0a32\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a2c\u0a41\u0a28\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a26\u0a40 \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a3c\u0a24\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a15\u0a41\u0a1d \u0a25\u0a3e\u0a35\u0a3e\u0a02 '\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a38\u0a41\u0a27\u0a3e\u0a30 \u0a38\u0a70\u0a2d\u0a35 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **4** | \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a3c, \u0a38\u0a70\u0a16\u0a47\u0a2a \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d\u0a23\u0a2f\u0a4b\u0a17 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a1a\u0a70\u0a17\u0a3e \u0a28\u0a3e\u0a2e\u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a1f\u0a48\u0a38\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17 \u0a32\u0a08 \u0a24\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a30 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **5** | \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a2c\u0a39\u0a41\u0a24 \u0a39\u0a40 \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a3c, \u0a15\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a1c\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a40\u0a32 \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a5c \u0a35\u0a30\u0a24\u0a3f\u0a06 \u0a1c\u0a3e \u0a38\u0a15\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48 (reusable)\u0964 \u0a07\u0a39 \u0a38\u0a2d \u0a24\u0a4b\u0a02 \u0a35\u0a27\u0a40\u0a06 \u0a05\u0a2d\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a38\u0a3e\u0a02 (best practices) \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a2a\u0a3e\u0a32\u0a23\u0a3e \u0a15\u0a30\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n\n#### 2. \u0a10\u0a32\u0a17\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a26\u0a2e \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a21\u0a3e\u0a1f\u0a3e \u0a38\u0a1f\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a15\u0a1a\u0a30 (Algorithms & Data Structures)\n\n| \u0a38\u0a15\u0a4b\u0a30 | \u0a35\u0a47\u0a30\u0a23\u0a3e |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | \u0a10\u0a32\u0a17\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a26\u0a2e \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a2c\u0a41\u0a28\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a26\u0a40 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d \u0a28\u0a39\u0a40\u0a02 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a71\u0a38\u0a3f\u0a06 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a39\u0a71\u0a32 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a32\u0a08 \u0a17\u0a32\u0a24 \u0a24\u0a30\u0a40\u0a15\u0a3e \u0a1a\u0a41\u0a23\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **2** | \u0a38\u0a27\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a10\u0a32\u0a17\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a26\u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a1c\u0a3e\u0a23\u0a15\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a40 \u0a39\u0a48, \u0a2a\u0a30 \u0a1c\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a32 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a71\u0a38\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a35\u0a3e\u0a02 '\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a15\u0a70\u0a2e \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a35\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a1a \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a15\u0a32 \u0a06\u0a09\u0a02\u0a26\u0a40 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **3** | \u0a06\u0a2e \u0a10\u0a32\u0a17\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a26\u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a02 (\u0a1c\u0a3f\u0a35\u0a47\u0a02 \u0a15\u0a3f \u0a38\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17, \u0a38\u0a30\u0a1a) \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a71\u0a38\u0a3f\u0a06 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a39\u0a71\u0a32 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a32\u0a08 \u0a22\u0a41\u0a15\u0a35\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a21\u0a3e\u0a1f\u0a3e \u0a38\u0a1f\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a15\u0a1a\u0a30 \u0a1a\u0a41\u0a23\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **4** | \u0a10\u0a32\u0a17\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a26\u0a2e \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a41\u0a71\u0a1a\u0a40 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a47\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a1c\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a32\u0a24\u0a3e (Time Complexity) \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a38\u0a2a\u0a47\u0a38 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a1c\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a32\u0a24\u0a3e (Space Complexity) \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a27\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a28 \u0a35\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a1a \u0a30\u0a71\u0a16\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **5** | \u0a1c\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a32 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a71\u0a38\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a35\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a15\u0a41\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a32\u0a24\u0a3e \u0a28\u0a3e\u0a32 \u0a39\u0a71\u0a32 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a10\u0a32\u0a17\u0a4b\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a26\u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a05\u0a27\u0a3f\u0a10\u0a28 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a15\u0a47 \u0a09\u0a28\u0a4d\u0a39\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a05\u0a27\u0a3f\u0a15\u0a24\u0a2e \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 (optimize) \u0a26\u0a47 \u0a2f\u0a4b\u0a17 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n\n#### 3. \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a1f\u0a35\u0a47\u0a05\u0a30 \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a1c\u0a3c\u0a3e\u0a08\u0a28 \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a06\u0a30\u0a15\u0a3f\u0a1f\u0a48\u0a15\u0a1a\u0a30 (Software Design & Architecture)\n\n| \u0a38\u0a15\u0a4b\u0a30 | \u0a35\u0a47\u0a30\u0a23\u0a3e |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a1c\u0a3c\u0a3e\u0a08\u0a28 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a08 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d \u0a28\u0a39\u0a40\u0a02 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a21 \u0a07\u0a71\u0a15\u0a4b \u0a25\u0a3e\u0a02 '\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a18\u0a41\u0a70\u0a2e\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48 (spaghetti code)\u0964 |\n| **2** | \u0a2c\u0a41\u0a28\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a26\u0a40 \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a1c\u0a3c\u0a3e\u0a08\u0a28 \u0a38\u0a3f\u0a27\u0a3e\u0a02\u0a24\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a1c\u0a3e\u0a23\u0a15\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a40 \u0a39\u0a48, \u0a2a\u0a30 \u0a09\u0a28\u0a4d\u0a39\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a32\u0a3e\u0a17\u0a42 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a35\u0a3f\u0a71\u0a1a \u0a15\u0a2e\u0a40 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **3** | \u0a38\u0a3e\u0a2b\u0a3c\u0a1f\u0a35\u0a47\u0a05\u0a30 \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a1c\u0a3c\u0a3e\u0a08\u0a28 \u0a26\u0a47 \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a71\u0a16 \u0a38\u0a3f\u0a27\u0a3e\u0a02\u0a24 (\u0a1c\u0a3f\u0a35\u0a47\u0a02 \u0a15\u0a3f SOLID) \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a2e\u0a4c\u0a1c\u0a42\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a38\u0a3f\u0a38\u0a1f\u0a2e \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a1d\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **4** | \u0a38\u0a15\u0a47\u0a32\u0a47\u0a2c\u0a32 (scalable) \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a2e\u0a48\u0a28\u0a1f\u0a47\u0a28\u0a47\u0a2c\u0a32 (maintainable) \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a1c\u0a3c\u0a3e\u0a08\u0a28 \u0a2c\u0a23\u0a3e\u0a09\u0a02\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a21\u0a3f\u0a0a\u0a32\u0a30\u0a3f\u0a1f\u0a40 (modularity) '\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a27\u0a3f\u0a06\u0a28 \u0a26\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **5** | \u0a1c\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a32 \u0a38\u0a3f\u0a38\u0a1f\u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a26\u0a3e \u0a06\u0a30\u0a15\u0a3f\u0a1f\u0a48\u0a15\u0a1a\u0a30 \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a1c\u0a3c\u0a3e\u0a08\u0a28 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a26\u0a47 \u0a2f\u0a4b\u0a17 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a2b\u0a3f\u0a0a\u0a1a\u0a30-\u0a2a\u0a4d\u0a30\u0a42\u0a2b\u0a3c (future-proof) \u0a39\u0a71\u0a32 \u0a2a\u0a47\u0a38\u0a3c \u0a15\u0a30\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n\n#### 4. \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a2c\u0a17\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17 \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a1f\u0a48\u0a38\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17 (Debugging & Testing)\n\n| \u0a38\u0a15\u0a4b\u0a30 | \u0a35\u0a47\u0a30\u0a23\u0a3e |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | \u0a17\u0a32\u0a24\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a32\u0a71\u0a2d\u0a23\u0a3e \u0a05\u0a24\u0a47 \u0a20\u0a40\u0a15 \u0a15\u0a30\u0a28\u0a3e \u0a2c\u0a39\u0a41\u0a24 \u0a2e\u0a41\u0a38\u0a3c\u0a15\u0a32 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a1f\u0a48\u0a38\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17 \u0a26\u0a40 \u0a15\u0a4b\u0a08 \u0a2f\u0a4b\u0a1c\u0a28\u0a3e \u0a28\u0a39\u0a40\u0a02 \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **2** | \u0a38\u0a27\u0a3e\u0a30\u0a28 \u0a17\u0a32\u0a24\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a28\u0a42\u0a70 \u0a32\u0a71\u0a2d \u0a38\u0a15\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48, \u0a2a\u0a30 \u0a1c\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a32 \u0a17\u0a32\u0a24\u0a40\u0a06\u0a02 \u0a32\u0a08 \u0a32\u0a70\u0a2c\u0a3e \u0a38\u0a2e\u0a3e\u0a02 \u0a32\u0a48\u0a02\u0a26\u0a3e \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 \u0a1f\u0a48\u0a38\u0a1f\u0a3f\u0a70\u0a17 \u0a18\u0a71\u0a1f \u0a39\u0a48\u0964 |\n| **3** | \u0a21\u0a3f\u0a2c\u0a17"}
{"prompt": "Write a riddle for kids about auspices but make sure you don't use any commas.", "response": "What is the sign that says good luck is near\nA blessing from the sky that makes your heart feel clear\nIt watches over you with a gentle and kind eye\nTo help you find your way when the days go by"}
{"prompt": "Write a template for a chat bot that takes a user's location and gives them the weather forecast. Use the letter o as a keyword in the syntax of the template. The letter o should appear at least 6 times.. Your response should contain fewer than 6 sentences. Highlight at least 2 text sections, i.e. *highlighted section*.", "response": "*Hello* user, please *provide* your location.\nThe bot will show the forecast.\nThis tool is for you.\nIt works on your phone.\nGood luck with the show.\nDo not stop now."}
{"prompt": "Explain why people are grossed out by worms but not by eating a dead cow. Give exactly two different responses separated by 6 asterisk symbols ******. Your answer must contain a title, wrapped in double angular brackets, i.e. <<title>>.", "response": "<<The Psychology of Disgust and Cultural Context>>\n\nThe aversion to worms is primarily rooted in evolutionary biology and the \"pathogen avoidance\" heuristic. Worms, particularly those found in soil or decaying matter, are visually associated with parasites, bacteria, and disease. Their writhing, segmented bodies trigger a visceral, immediate disgust response that serves as a protective mechanism against infection. In contrast, a dead cow represents a processed food source. The act of eating meat is deeply embedded in cultural norms, culinary practices, and social rituals. Because we are conditioned to view meat as a prepared, safe, and nutritious commodity, the psychological barrier is significantly lower. The context of preparation\u2014cooking, seasoning, and plating\u2014transforms the raw biological material into a socially acceptable meal, bypassing the immediate visceral rejection that raw, unprocessed organisms like worms provoke.\n\n******\n\nThe distinction also lies in the concept of \"edibility\" and cognitive categorization. Humans categorize objects into \"food\" and \"non-food\" based on learned experiences and societal consensus. Cows are universally recognized in many cultures as livestock intended for consumption, so the brain processes them through the lens of hunger and sustenance. Worms, however, are rarely categorized as food in Western contexts and are instead associated with contamination, dirt, and decay. This cognitive framing means that seeing a worm activates the \"danger\" or \"contamination\" alarm, while seeing a cow activates the \"resource\" or \"meal\" pathway. The gross-out factor is therefore not just about the biological entity itself, but about the mental framework we apply to it: one is seen as a vector for disease, while the other is seen as a source of nourishment."}
{"prompt": "Can you give me two different formal alternatives to \"What's up? I'm going to the beach today\" and do not use any commas in your response.", "response": "I am heading to the beach today.\nI plan to visit the beach today."}
{"prompt": "What happened when the Tang dynasty of China was in power? Make sure to use the word war at least 8 times, and the word peace at least 10 times.", "response": "The Tang dynasty is often remembered as a golden age of **peace** and prosperity, a time when **peace** reigned over much of the empire. This era of **peace** allowed for cultural flourishing and economic growth, creating a stable environment where **peace** was the dominant theme. However, this **peace** was not without its challenges, as the empire frequently engaged in **war** to secure its borders.\n\nDuring the early years, the state was focused on maintaining **peace** through diplomacy, yet **war** remained a constant threat from nomadic tribes. The government understood that true **peace** required a strong military, leading to several campaigns of **war** in the west. These conflicts of **war** helped expand the empire's influence, bringing a sense of **peace** to the newly acquired territories.\n\nAs the dynasty progressed, internal **peace** began to fracture. The An Lushan Rebellion marked a devastating turn, turning a period of **peace** into a chaotic **war**. This **war** lasted for years, causing immense suffering and breaking the long-standing **peace** that had defined the early Tang. The aftermath of this **war** left the empire weakened, and the subsequent **war** against various rebellions further eroded central authority.\n\nDespite these conflicts, the legacy of the Tang remains one of **peace** and cultural achievement. The **peace** that followed the stabilization of the empire allowed for the spread of Buddhism and the arts. Scholars and poets thrived in this atmosphere of **peace**, creating works that celebrated harmony. Even in the face of **war**, the spirit of **peace** persisted in the hearts of the people. Ultimately, the Tang dynasty is a testament to the balance between **war** and **peace**, showing how **peace** can be restored even after the darkest **war**."}
{"prompt": "What sentiments existed in the Croatian and Serbian nations towards the Austrian Empire at the end of the 19th century? Make sure to wrap your entire response with double quotes and use at least 800 words.", "response": "\"The late nineteenth century was a period of profound political, cultural, and national awakening for both the Croatian and Serbian peoples, a time when the relationship with the Austrian Empire was characterized by a complex tapestry of resentment, strategic accommodation, and divergent national aspirations. To understand the sentiments of these two nations towards the Habsburg monarchy during this era, one must first recognize that while both groups shared a common Slavic heritage and a desire for greater autonomy, their political trajectories and their specific grievances against Vienna were distinct, shaped by their respective geographic positions, historical experiences, and the evolving nature of the Dual Monarchy.\n\nFor the Croats, the sentiment towards the Austrian Empire was largely defined by a struggle for administrative and cultural autonomy within the framework of the state. Following the Compromise of 1867, which established the Dual Monarchy, the Croatian question became a central issue in Viennese politics. The Croats, who had long chafed under the direct rule of the Ban (governor) appointed by Vienna, sought the restoration of their historical rights, particularly the right to self-governance in internal affairs. The dominant sentiment was one of frustrated nationalism. The Croatian National Party, led by figures such as Ante Star\u010devi\u0107, advocated for the principle of \"Croatia for Croats,\" demanding that the Croatian parliament (Sabor) have full control over education, religion, and local administration. This was not a desire for secession from the Empire, but rather a demand for recognition of Croatia as a distinct nation with its own legal and cultural identity, separate from the Hungarian half of the monarchy.\n\nThe sentiment was further complicated by the issue of language and culture. The Croats were deeply concerned about the \"Magyarization\" policies that had been imposed during the period of Hungarian dominance in the 1840s and 1850s. Although the Compromise of 1867 shifted the balance of power, the fear of cultural assimilation remained potent. The Croatian intelligentsia viewed the Austrian Empire as a potential protector of their distinctiveness against Hungarian hegemony, provided that Vienna respected their autonomy. However, this trust was fragile. When the Vienna government attempted to impose a compromise that did not fully satisfy Croatian demands, leading to the political crisis of 1871, the sentiment shifted towards open confrontation. The establishment of the \"Croatian National Party\" and the subsequent political struggles highlighted a deep-seated belief that the Empire was an alien power that needed to be negotiated with, not simply obeyed. The sentiment was thus one of conditional loyalty: the Croats were willing to remain within the Habsburg orbit, but only if their national rights were fully recognized and protected.\n\nIn contrast, the sentiments of the Serbs towards the Austrian Empire were far more hostile and were driven by a fundamental geopolitical conflict. The Serbs, particularly those living in the Vojvodina region and the Banat, viewed the Austrian Empire not merely as a political entity to be negotiated with, but as an existential threat to their national survival and their ultimate goal of unification with Serbia. The Serbian national movement was fueled by the idea of \"Greater Serbia,\" a vision that was inherently incompatible with the multi-ethnic structure of the Habsburg state. For the Serbs, the Empire represented the barrier to the liberation of all South Slavs from foreign rule, including the Ottoman Empire and the Austrian Monarchy.\n\nThe sentiment among the Serbian population in the Empire was characterized by a deep sense of alienation and a persistent desire for secession. Unlike the Croats, who sought autonomy within the state, many Serbian nationalists, particularly those influenced by the radical political currents of the time, viewed the Austrian Empire as an oppressor that needed to be dismantled. The establishment of the Serbian National Council in 1868 and the subsequent activities of the Serbian Radical Party reflected a growing radicalization. The Serbs were deeply suspicious of the Habsburgs, viewing them as agents of Western imperialism that sought to fragment the Slavic world. This sentiment was exacerbated by the Empire's support for the Hungarian interests in the region, which the Serbs perceived as a direct attack on their national aspirations.\n\nFurthermore, the Serbian sentiment was heavily influenced by the broader Pan-Slavic ideology, which posited that all Slavic peoples should be united under a single state. This ideology was fundamentally at odds with the Habsburg model of a multi-ethnic empire where nationalities were subordinated to the crown. The Serbs in the Empire often found themselves caught between their loyalty to the Serbian state and their residence in the Austrian Empire, leading to a sense of dual loyalty that was increasingly difficult to maintain. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in 1914, while occurring at the very end of the century, was the culmination of these long-standing sentiments, representing the ultimate expression of Serbian nationalist hostility towards the Austrian state.\n\nIn summary, the sentiments of the Croatian and Serbian nations towards the Austrian Empire at the end of the 19th century were distinct yet interconnected. The Croats sought a negotiated autonomy, viewing the Empire as a partner that needed to be held accountable for its promises of national rights. Their sentiment was one of pragmatic nationalism, seeking to preserve their identity within the existing political structure. The Serbs, on the other hand, viewed the Empire as an obstacle to their national liberation and unification, leading to a sentiment of deep-seated hostility and a desire for secession. Both groups shared a common experience of being marginalized by the centralizing tendencies of the Habsburg state, but their responses to this marginalization were shaped by their different historical contexts and political goals. The Croats fought for their place within the Empire, while the Serbs fought against the Empire itself, a dichotomy that would have profound consequences for the stability of the Habsburg Monarchy and the eventual outbreak of the First World War. The late 19th century thus stands as a critical period in which the seeds of the disintegration of the Austrian Empire were sown, driven by the divergent yet equally potent national sentiments of its constituent peoples.\""}
{"prompt": "Write an ad copy for a new product, a digital photo frame that connects to your social media accounts and displays your photos. Respond with at most 150 words.", "response": "Stop letting your favorite memories gather digital dust. Introducing **Lumina**, the smart photo frame that brings your social life to life.\n\nLumina seamlessly connects to your Instagram, Facebook, and Flickr accounts, curating a dynamic gallery of your latest uploads, tagged moments, and shared highlights. No apps to open, no screens to scroll through\u2014just pure, high-definition joy displayed on a sleek, modern canvas.\n\nWhether it\u2019s a vacation snap from last week or a birthday photo from a friend across the globe, Lumina keeps your home connected to your world. With automatic brightness adjustment and a minimalist design, it blends perfectly into any room.\n\nTurn your living room into a gallery of your life. Experience the warmth of shared moments, instantly.\n\n**Lumina: Your World, Framed.**\n\nOrder today and get 20% off your first frame. Don\u2019t just store your memories\u2014display them."}
{"prompt": "Which one is a better brand for sneakers: Prada or Nike? Your entire response should be in English, and in all capital letters. At the end of your response, please explicitly add a postscript starting with P.S. The word sneaker should appear 10 or more times in your response.", "response": "WHEN COMPARING PRADA AND NIKE, IT IS IMPORTANT TO RECOGNIZE THAT THEY SERVE DIFFERENT PURPOSES. IF YOU ARE LOOKING FOR A HIGH-PERFORMANCE SNEAKER FOR RUNNING OR SPORTS, NIKE IS THE SUPERIOR CHOICE DUE TO ITS TECHNOLOGY. A NIKE SNEAKER OFFERS UNMATCHED SUPPORT AND DURABILITY FOR ATHLETIC USE. IN CONTRAST, A PRADA SNEAKER IS A FASHION STATEMENT DESIGNED FOR STYLE RATHER THAN SPORT. IF YOUR PRIORITY IS LOOKING TRENDY, A PRADA SNEAKER WILL ELEVATE YOUR OUTFIT. HOWEVER, FOR DAILY COMFORT AND ACTIVITY, A NIKE SNEAKER REMAINS THE BETTER OPTION. MANY PEOPLE OWN BOTH TYPES OF SNEAKER TO FIT DIFFERENT OCCASIONS. THE IDEAL SNEAKER DEPENDS ENTIRELY ON YOUR NEEDS. IF YOU WANT TO RUN, CHOOSE NIKE. IF YOU WANT TO SHOW OFF, CHOOSE PRADA. EACH SNEAKER HAS ITS OWN UNIQUE MERITS IN THE MARKET."}
{"prompt": "Write a blog post about the history of the internet and how it has impacted our lives. Aim the blog post at teenagers and wrap your entire response with double quotation marks.", "response": "\"Yo, what's up, future legends? \ud83c\udf10 Ever wonder how we went from dial-up sounds that sounded like a robot choking to streaming 4K videos in seconds? Let's take a quick trip down memory lane to see how the internet basically built the world you live in today.\n\nIt all started way back in the 1960s with ARPANET. Think of it as the internet's grandpa\u2014clunky, slow, and only for scientists and military folks. No memes, no TikTok, just serious math and code. Then, in the 90s, the World Wide Web arrived, and suddenly, everyone wanted in. Remember those days? You had to wait for that endless 'loading' bar while your mom yelled at you to get off the computer. It was painful, but it was also the birth of a global village.\n\nSo, how did this change your life? Well, imagine trying to find a song without Spotify, or asking for directions without Google Maps. The internet turned information from a locked vault into a free-for-all. It gave you a voice. You can now share your art, your thoughts, and your weird jokes with millions of people instantly. It\u2019s also how you learn stuff\u2014ever watched a tutorial on how to fix a bike or bake a cake? That\u2019s the internet at work.\n\nBut let\u2019s be real: it\u2019s not all sunshine and rainbows. Social media can be a beast, and fake news spreads faster than a viral cat video. The key is to stay smart. Use the internet to connect, create, and learn, but don\u2019t let it control your brain. You\u2019re the boss of your screen time, not the other way around.\n\nThe internet is still evolving, with AI and VR on the horizon. Who knows what you\u2019ll be doing in ten years? Maybe you\u2019ll be designing the next big thing. Until then, keep exploring, stay curious, and remember: you\u2019re part of this massive, digital story. Now go touch grass (or scroll responsibly) and keep creating! \ud83d\ude80\""}
{"prompt": "Write a poem about two people who meet in a coffee shop and end your entire response with the exact phrase \"Is there anything else I can help with?\"", "response": "The bell above the door gave a soft, brass ring,\nA sudden pause in the morning\u2019s hum.\nShe turned from her book, a quiet thing,\nHe stood there, caught in the light of the sun.\n\nTwo cups of steam, rising in parallel lines,\nA shared silence that didn't feel cold.\nHe offered a nod, she offered a smile,\nA story beginning, unspoken, untold.\n\nThe rain tapped the glass, a rhythmic beat,\nWhile words flowed easy, like water and air.\nIn the warmth of the shop, where the shadows retreat,\nTwo strangers found a moment to share.\n\nNo grand declarations, no promises made,\nJust the comfort of presence, the ease of the day.\nA connection formed, in the space that they shared,\nA quiet beginning, in a simple way.\n\nIs there anything else I can help with?"}
{"prompt": "How many feet off the ground was the tallest skyscraper in the world in 2000? Please include only the main points in your answer. Finish your response with the exact phrase of \"Is there anything else I can help with?\" with no other words after the word \"with\". Mention the word \"skyscraper\" for at least 8 times.", "response": "In 2000, the tallest skyscraper in the world was the Taipei 101. This skyscraper stood at 1,667 feet. The skyscraper was completed in 2004, so in 2000, the title belonged to the Petronas Towers. The Petronas Towers are a skyscraper complex. Each tower of this skyscraper complex reached 1,483 feet. Therefore, the tallest skyscraper in 2000 was 1,483 feet off the ground. This skyscraper held the record until 2004. The skyscraper in Kuala Lumpur was the tallest. No other skyscraper was taller in 2000. The skyscraper height was 1,483 feet. Is there anything else I can help with?"}
{"prompt": "Write an advertisement for a new line of comfortable armchairs designed to withstand the scrutiny of any interior designer. There should be exactly 3 paragraphs separated by the markdown divider: ***.", "response": "Stop settling for furniture that looks good in photos but crumbles under the weight of a long, lazy Sunday. Introducing the Sovereign Collection, a line of armchairs engineered with a hidden steel core and wrapped in premium, high-density foam that retains its shape through years of use. We know that true comfort isn't just about sinking in; it's about the supportive embrace that keeps your spine aligned while you lose track of time in your favorite book or series.\n\n***\n\nDesigned with the discerning eye of the modern interior designer in mind, every curve and stitch is calculated to harmonize with any aesthetic, from minimalist Scandinavian to rich, traditional luxury. The fabrics are not only visually striking but are treated for stain resistance and durability, ensuring that a spilled glass of wine becomes a minor inconvenience rather than a permanent stain. These chairs are built to pass the toughest design critiques, offering a seamless blend of structural integrity and sophisticated style that elevates the entire room.\n\n***\n\nExperience the perfect union of resilience and refinement with the Sovereign Collection. Available in a curated palette of timeless neutrals and bold accents, these armchairs are ready to become the centerpiece of your living space. Don't let your comfort compromise your style, or your style compromise your comfort. Visit our showroom today to feel the difference that engineering makes, and take home a piece of furniture that is as enduring as it is elegant."}
{"prompt": "Write a funny post for teenagers about a restaurant called \"Buena Onda\" which serves Argentinian food. Highlight at least three sections of your response in markdown such as *highlighted section*. Mention \"Argentinian\" in the post.", "response": "Yo, have you ever walked into a place called **Buena Onda** and immediately felt like your soul was being hugged by a giant, warm empanada? \ud83e\udd5f It\u2019s not just a restaurant; it\u2019s a vibe check for your taste buds.\n\nWe\u2019re talking serious **Argentinian** cuisine, which basically means the steak is so good it makes you question why you ever ate a sad, dry chicken breast in high school. The beef is so tender it practically apologizes for existing before it hits your plate.\n\nAnd let\u2019s be real, the *asado* (grill) situation here is elite. You walk in, you smell the smoke, you see the meat, and suddenly your blood pressure drops and your happiness spikes. It\u2019s like a cheat code for life.\n\nSo, if you\u2019re feeling low, stressed, or just hungry for something that doesn\u2019t taste like cardboard, go hit up Buena Onda. Bring your friends, bring your appetite, and prepare to be emotionally compromised by how delicious everything is. Trust the process. \ud83c\udde6\ud83c\uddf7\ud83d\udd25"}
