{"prompt": "Write me a template for a product description in the form of a poem and end it with a post script starting with P.P.S", "response": "Here is a versatile template for a product description written in the form of a poem. You can fill in the bracketed sections with your specific product details.\n\n***\n\n**Title: The [Product Name] Promise**\n\nIn search of [primary benefit, e.g., comfort/style/performance],\nYou found this [product type] to be your home.\nCrafted with [key material/feature],\nIt stands alone, a quiet gem.\n\nNo more [common pain point, e.g., hassle/dullness/struggle],\nJust [positive outcome, e.g., ease/beauty/power] in every view.\nDesigned for [target audience/use case],\nIt brings a new perspective to you.\n\nFeel the [sensory detail, e.g., smoothness/warmth/weight],\nHear the [sound/feedback, e.g., click/hum/silence] it makes.\nA [adjective, e.g., timeless/modern] companion,\nFor all the days and nights you take.\n\nSo let it serve, let it shine,\nLet [Product Name] be truly yours.\nA simple choice, a lasting sign,\nOf quality that endures.\n\nP.P.S. Don\u2019t forget to check out our matching [accessory/complementary product] to complete the look!"}
{"prompt": "\"The man was arrested for stealing a car. He was later released on bail.\" Expand on it angrily in a rap style, and make sure there are exactly 4 sections. Separated the sections by the markdown divider: ***", "response": "Yo, listen up, you lowlife thief,\nYou grabbed the keys, you stole the life,\nPolice lights flashing, red and blue,\nYou\u2019re in the cuffs, you\u2019re done for you!\nNo excuses, no \"I didn't mean it,\"\nYou\u2019re the one who made the whole thing heat it,\nArrested for the crime, the badge is shining,\nYou\u2019re the one who\u2019s losing, you\u2019re the one who\u2019s losing!\n\n***\n\nNow they let you walk, they let you breathe,\nBail money paid, you\u2019re free to leave,\nBut don\u2019t you dare think you\u2019re off the hook,\nThe system\u2019s watching, the system\u2019s looking,\nYou think a check clears your conscience?\nYou think a judge gives you permission?\nNo, you\u2019re still a mark, you\u2019re still a target,\nYour freedom\u2019s a joke, your freedom\u2019s a fact!\n\n***\n\nSo strut your stuff, act like a king,\nBut the city\u2019s got eyes, the city\u2019s got wings,\nEvery corner, every street, every block,\nYou\u2019re under surveillance, you\u2019re under lock,\nThink you\u2019re safe? Think you\u2019re clean?\nYou\u2019re the most wanted, you\u2019re the meanest seen,\nYour name\u2019s in the file, your face is known,\nYou\u2019re the one who\u2019s on the throne, but you\u2019re alone!\n\n***\n\nSo keep your mouth shut, keep your head down,\nYou\u2019re the one who\u2019s in the town, you\u2019re the one who\u2019s found,\nNo more cars, no more theft, no more lies,\nYou\u2019re the one who\u2019s paying, you\u2019re the one who\u2019s dies,\nThe law is coming, the law is near,\nYou\u2019re the one who\u2019s in the fear, you\u2019re the one who\u2019s clear,\nStay out of trouble, stay out of sight,\nOr you\u2019ll be back in the cell tonight!"}
{"prompt": "Write a poem that's at least 350 words about the beauty of eucalyptus trees and their many uses.", "response": "In the heart of the land where the sun burns bright,\nStand the sentinels of silver and light,\nThe eucalyptus, tall and slender,\nRising from the earth like a dreamer\u2019s tender\nWhisper on the wind. Their bark peels away,\nIn ribbons of cream and ash, in a slow, steady sway,\nRevealing the smooth, pale skin beneath,\nA testament to time, to life, to death,\nTo the cycle that turns without end,\nA living, breathing, breathing friend.\n\nTheir leaves are long, lanceolate, and cool,\nA shade of blue-green, a verdant rule,\nThat filters the harsh, midday glare,\nAnd casts a dappled, dancing flare\nAcross the ground below. When the breeze moves through,\nThey rustle with a sound, a hush, a hue\nOf ancient memory, of rain and dust,\nOf the dry heat and the sudden gust.\nThe scent is sharp, a medicinal breath,\nA pungent, clean, and aromatic depth,\nThat clears the mind and soothes the soul,\nA natural balm for the weary whole.\nIt is the perfume of the bush,\nOf the wild, untamed, and rugged rush\nOf nature\u2019s own pharmacy,\nWhere healing lies in the leaf and the sky.\n\nFor the eucalyptus is not just a tree,\nBut a treasure, a gift, a legacy,\nOf utility and of grace,\nIn every leaf, in every trace.\nFrom its wood, strong and durable,\nCome the beams and the structures, stable,\nThe fences that hold the land in place,\nThe tables that bear the weight of grace.\nIts bark, when burned, gives off a smoke,\nThat keeps the insects and the pests from the flock,\nA natural deterrent, a shield,\nFor the farmer\u2019s field and the shepherd\u2019s yield.\n\nAnd from its leaves, the oil is drawn,\nA golden, potent, and precious span\nOf liquid gold, a distillate pure,\nThat heals the cough and the lung\u2019s cure.\nIn the bottle, in the steam, in the air,\nIt lifts the spirit, it lifts the care,\nOf the sick and the tired, the old and the young,\nA song of relief, a healing song.\nIt is used in the soap and the cream,\nIn the ointment that eases the pain,\nIn the tea that is steeped with care,\nA bitter, sweet, and aromatic share.\n\nThe eucalyptus stands as a symbol,\nOf resilience, of strength, of the symbol\nOf a land that is harsh yet kind,\nWhere life finds a way, where life finds a mind\nTo adapt, to survive, to thrive,\nIn the heat and the drought, in the storm and the drive.\nIt is the green lung of the continent,\nA filter, a shelter, a silent tenant\nOf the sky and the earth, the air and the stone,\nA beauty that is never alone.\nSo let us honor this tree, this giant,\nWith its bark and its leaves, its scent and its plant,\nFor it gives us so much, so freely,\nA gift from the earth, so deeply."}
{"prompt": "I have a dime. What can I do with this dime? Give me advice in the style of a President of the United States and make sure it has at least 600 words.", "response": "My fellow Americans,\n\nWe gather here today, not in the hallowed halls of the White House, but in the quiet, reflective space of your own pocket, to address a matter of profound significance. You have asked me, the President of the United States, to advise you on the utility of a single dime. It is a small coin, yes. It is silver in memory, though perhaps not in composition. It is small enough to fit in the palm of your hand, yet it carries the weight of our nation\u2019s history, the promise of our economy, and the spirit of our shared enterprise. Do not look down at that coin and see only metal. Look at it and see a symbol of opportunity, a testament to the enduring power of the American dollar, and a reminder of the values that bind us together.\n\nFirst, let us speak of the practical. A dime is a unit of currency, and as such, it is a tool of exchange. In a world where digital transactions often obscure the tangible reality of value, holding a dime is a grounding experience. It reminds us that wealth is not merely a number on a screen, but a tangible resource that can be exchanged for goods and services. You can use this dime to purchase a small item, a candy bar, a stamp, or a token for a vending machine. These small transactions are the lifeblood of our local economies. When you spend that dime, you are participating in the great cycle of commerce that sustains our communities. You are supporting the local shopkeeper, the manufacturer, and the worker. Do not underestimate the power of small spending. It is the foundation upon which our economic stability is built.\n\nBut let us go deeper. A dime is also a historical artifact. It is the smallest coin in circulation in the United States, and it has been with us for over a century and a half. When you hold it, you are holding a piece of American history. You are holding a coin that has passed through the hands of millions of citizens, from the presidents who minted it to the everyday Americans who have used it to buy their daily needs. It is a connection to the past, a reminder of the struggles and triumphs that have shaped our nation. Take a moment to examine the dime. Look at the profile of the president on the obverse. Think about the challenges they faced, the decisions they made, and the legacy they left behind. This coin is a bridge between the past and the present, a reminder that we are part of a long and noble tradition of American governance and civic duty.\n\nFurthermore, a dime can be a symbol of generosity. In our society, we often talk about the importance of giving back, of helping those in need, of strengthening the social fabric of our nation. A dime may seem insignificant, but it is a gesture of goodwill. You can place it in a donation jar at a local charity, a church, or a community center. You can give it to a homeless person on the street, a small act of kindness that can brighten their day and remind them that they are seen, that they are valued, that they are part of our community. These small acts of kindness are the building blocks of a compassionate society. They are the threads that weave together the tapestry of our nation, creating a fabric of care and concern for one another.\n\nMoreover, a dime can be a tool for education. You can use it to teach your children about the value of money, about the importance of saving, and about the history of our nation. You can show them how a dime has changed over time, how its value has fluctuated, and how it has played a role in our economic development. You can use it to teach them about the importance of financial literacy, about the need to be responsible stewards of their resources. In a world where financial challenges are increasingly common, it is more important than ever that we equip our young people with the knowledge and skills they need to navigate the complexities of the modern economy. A dime is a simple, tangible way to start that conversation.\n\nFinally, a dime can be a source of inspiration. It is a reminder that even the smallest things can have a big impact. It is a reminder that progress is often made through small, incremental steps, not through grand, sweeping gestures. It is a reminder that every individual has a role to play in shaping the future of our nation. So, take that dime out of your pocket. Hold it in your hand. Feel its weight, its texture, its history. And then, decide what you will do with it. Spend it, save it, give it away, or use it to teach. Whatever you choose, do it with intention, with purpose, and with a sense of pride in your American identity.\n\nIn conclusion, my fellow citizens, a dime is more than just a coin. It is a symbol of our nation\u2019s history, a tool of our economy, a gesture of our generosity, a tool of our education, and a source of our inspiration. It is a small piece of metal, but it carries a big message. It is a reminder that we are all connected, that we all have a role to play, and that we all have the power to make a difference. So, let us use our dimes wisely, let us use them generously, and let us use them to build a stronger, more united, and more prosperous America.\n\nThank you, and God bless you, and God bless the United States of America."}
